\par\par \uc2 THE HUNT\par \uc2 Real Estate Desk; SECTRE\par \b \uc2 Work From Home in a 175-Square-Foot Studio? It's Time for a Move.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joyce Cohen \par \uc2 958 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 April 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Shortly after Katherine Campagna graduated from \uc2 Cornell University\uc2  three years ago, she and a roommate split a one-bedroom rental into a two-bedroom, on the fifth floor of a Lower East Side walk-up. She walked to her Battery Park City office, where she worked as a financial analyst.\par \par \uc2 She later upgraded to a studio -- also a fifth-floor walk-up -- just next door. ''I didn't want to measure it until I moved out, because I didn't want to know how small it was,'' Ms. Campagna, 25, said.\par \par \uc2 The apartment was 175 square feet, and it had space-saving built-ins, including a platform with drawers that held the mattress.\par \par \uc2 After the pandemic began, she went home for a few months to the San Francisco area, working remotely. When she returned to the city, her lease was set to expire and her landlord let her pay $2,000 a month in a month-to-month arrangement.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Campagna worried about working in such a tiny space. From one direction, she could hear the neighbor's radio; from the other, it was the neighbor's children. She called it ''an R.V. camper of an apartment.''\par \par \uc2 ''I wanted a one-bedroom with a door,'' or else a large studio she could section off, she said. ''I needed a space where my bed was not also my couch was not also my desk.''\par \par \uc2 ''I spent two months with very specific filters on my StreetEasy account, refreshing over and over,'' she said. Her new budget was up to $2,500. As rents started to fall, she felt she was in a good negotiating position.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Campagna considered Brooklyn, where she figured she could get sufficient space, and where many of her friends had recently migrated. ''They move in with their boyfriend, and then move to Brooklyn and get a dog,'' she said. She also liked the West Village, which would make for a simpler commute when her office reopened.\par \par \uc2 Among her options:\par \par \uc2 1. Downtown Brooklyn Studio\par \par \uc2 This alcove studio was more than 500 square feet, with four closets and a dishwasher, in a 2005 doorman building with a courtyard. It was on the edge of Cobble Hill, a 20-minute subway trip to work. The rent, with concessions, was around $2,580.\par \par \uc2 2. West Village Studio\par \par \uc2 This large studio, over 500 square feet, was in a 1964 co-op building with a morning doorman. It had plenty of closet space and a view over a courtyard. The walk to work was 30 minutes, and the rent was $2,450.\par \par \uc2 3. West Village One-Bedroom\par \par \uc2 This one-bedroom was around 325 square feet. It faced the street, with a kitchen in a corner of the living room, a French door and a decorative fireplace. The walk to work was 40 minutes, and the rent was $2,650.\par \par \uc2 Her Choice\par \par \uc2 She decided to forgo Brooklyn. ''I couldn't stomach living that far from work,'' she said. Nor did she want to be dependent on the subway; she preferred to walk. (She is now working in her office a few days a week.)\par \par \uc2 The West Village studio was ideally situated, and big enough to add a partition. But it was rented quickly by someone else.\par \par \uc2 She arranged to see a back-facing unit on the first floor of the West Village building with the one-bedroom. It was smaller than she expected, with low ceilings. ''You couldn't open the bathroom door all the way because it would hit the toilet,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 But the building had another vacancy: an apartment that was slightly larger and on a higher floor, facing the street. She loved it.\par \par \uc2 ''Kat was very specific about the location,'' said the listing agent, Shiri Rahamim, of Highline Residential.\par \par \uc2 ''The apartment is very charming, very West Village-y,'' Ms. Rahamim said. ''It's a West Village kitchen, you know? Her living space is big enough you can fit a sectional couch. Her bedroom is on the smaller side, but it's not a shoe box.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Campagna negotiated the rent to $2,550, paid a $1,750 broker fee and arrived in the late summer. Her commute is a pleasant walk along the Hudson River Greenway.\par \par \uc2 She uses the fireplace as a bookcase. In the bedroom, where the door doesn't open all the way, ''there are layers of plaster and paint on the walls, which gives my walls an interesting texture that makes it impossible to hang pictures,'' she said. ''My floor is incredibly sloped, but I find it charming.''\par \par \uc2 The lively neighborhood includes a bar across the street and a brass band that plays on weekday evenings. ''I have had multiple people on work calls say, 'Hey, Kat, can you turn the music down?''' she said. ''You can hear everything on the street.''\par \par \uc2 Still, the apartment ''has been such a source of joy for me in the last few months of the pandemic,'' she added. ''I feel very lucky.''\par \par \uc2 Email thehunt@nytimes.com\par \par \uc2 The Renter: Katherine Campagna, who calls her old fifth-floor walk-up ''an R.V. camper of an apartment.'' (PHOTOGRAPH BY TOM SIBLEY FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); The Options: An alcove studio in Downtown Brooklyn was over 500 square feet, with four closets. The doorman building had a courtyard.; A large studio in the West Village had plenty of closet space and a courtyard view. The 1964 co-op building had a morning doorman.; A one-bedroom in the West Village was about 325 square feet. It faced the street and had a French door and a decorative fireplace. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210418eh4i00040\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
