\par\par \uc2 Metropolitan Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Here to Help; Vanessa Friedman Answers Your Style Questions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Vanessa Friedman \par \uc2 490 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 April 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Working from home has lessened my need to color my hair, so the grays are rooting up. Unfortunately, I have black hair, thus the contrast of colors is quite stark. Do I continue to color or cover my hair, or do I let it become natural? I am ''winter'' in the coloring chart, but I have a feeling I may turn into spring and have to make drastic changes in my work clothes if I let my hair change. What are your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 Jill, Houston\par \par \uc2 To color or not to color? This is a dilemma Hamlet would understand. When salons were closed during the pandemic, many of us had to face our original hair shade for the first time in years.\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, a return to the office is looming. It is true that darker hair can coordinate differently with, say, bright blues or reds than do blond or white-gray shades, but the real question is how does it make you feel and what message do you want to send?\par \par \uc2 Do you want, for example, to signal how much you may have been changed by your experiences over the last year? If so, new hair is a great way to do it. And changing your color is an even bigger statement than cutting your hair because hair color brings all sorts of assumptions (right or wrong) with it. That is true whether you are revealing your hair truth or opting to dye.\par \par \uc2 When I asked Bee Shapiro, who writes about beauty for The Times, what she thought about your question, she said, ''I think of hair color as a personality thing rather than an age or even a wardrobe thing.''\par \par \uc2 If you are considering going gray, she suggested that you ask yourself whether ''gray represents where you are in life right now? Is it the way you feel, and is it your personality?''\par \par \uc2 ''Some women can wear gray in their 20s, and it suits them,'' she went on, ''but I interviewed the designer \uc2 Norma Kamali\uc2  recently, and she is 75 and just does not 'feel gray,' so she colors her hair darker.''\par \par \uc2 On one hand, gray hair can indicate maturity and wisdom. But it can also communicate age -- or a lack of vanity. And irritating as it is, women with gray hair still get judged differently than men with gray hair. Maybe the pandemic gives us the opportunity to change that.\par \par \uc2 Or maybe, as the Prince of Denmark said, ''And thus the native hue of resolution/Is sicklied o'er with the pale cast of thought.''\par \par \uc2 Every week in the Open Thread newsletter -- a look from across The Times at the forces that shape the dress codes we share -- The Times's chief fashion critic, Vanessa Friedman, answers a reader's fashion-related question. Sign up for Open Thread at nytimes.com/newsletters.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210412eh4c0004g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
