\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Walks a Political Tightrope as Workers Vote on Union\b0\par\par\uc2 By David McCabe \par \uc2 1,535 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 March 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The company has always opposed efforts by its workers to organize, but its regulatory fate rests largely with Democrats in Washington.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is aligned with the Biden administration on several fronts.\par \par \uc2 It backs a $15-an-hour federal minimum wage. It has pledged to meet all the goals of the Paris climate agreement on reducing emissions. It has met with the administration to discuss how to help with the distribution of Covid-19 vaccines.\par \par \uc2 But a union drive at one of its warehouses in Alabama has the retailer doing a political balancing act: staying on the good side of Washington's Democratic leaders while squashing an organizing effort that President Biden has signaled his support for.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers in Bessemer, Ala., have been voting for weeks on whether to form a union. The voting ends Monday. Approval would be a first for \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers in the United States and could energize the labor movement across the country.\par \par \uc2 Labor organizers have tapped into dissatisfaction with working conditions in the warehouse, saying \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's pursuit of efficiency and profits makes the conditions harsh for workers. The company counters that its starting wage of $15 an hour exceeds what other employers in the area pay, and it has urged workers to vote against unionizing.\par \par \uc2 Labor leaders and liberal Democrats have seized on the union drive, saying it shows how \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is not as friendly to workers as the company says it is. Some of the company's critics are also using its resistance to the union push to argue that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  should not be trusted on other issues, like climate change and the federal minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has always fought against unionizing by its workers. But the vote in Alabama comes at a perilous moment for the company. Lawmakers and regulators -- not competitors -- are some of its greatest threats, and it has spent significant time and money trying to keep the government away from its business.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's business practices are the subject of antitrust investigations at the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  and in multiple state attorney general offices. Mr. Biden on Monday nominated Lina Khan, a legal scholar who came to prominence with her critique of the company, for a seat on the F.T.C.\par \par \uc2 ''I think everyone is seeing through the P.R. at this point and focusing on both their economic and political power,'' Sarah Miller, a critic of \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , said about the company. Ms. Miller, who runs the American Economic Liberties Project, an antitrust think tank, added, ''I think the narrative is cooked now on their status as a monopoly, their status as an abusive employer and their status as one of the biggest spenders on lobbying in Washington, D.C.''\par \par \uc2 Drew Herdener, Amazon's vice president for worldwide communications, said in a statement that the company shared common ground with the Biden administration on climate change, immigration reform, the minimum wage and pandemic policy, and was ''seeing really positive collaboration on those fronts'' with the White House.\par \par \uc2 ''As it relates to progressive Democrats in general, we've been surprised by some of the negative things we've seen certain members say in the press and on social media,'' he said. ''If there's a progressive company in this country, it's \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . Find me another large company paying two times the minimum wage, providing great health benefits from Day 1, 95 percent education reimbursement, safe working environment, and so on. We really think we are an example of what a U.S. company should be doing for its employees.''\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  spent $18.7 million on federal lobbying last year, compared with $2.1 million a decade ago, according to the Center for Responsive Politics. Its lobbyists press their employer's case on a variety of issues, like how drones are regulated and the laws that govern pharmacies.\par \par \uc2 Consumers adore \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . In late 2019, a national survey by The Verge, a technology news site, found that 91 percent had a favorable view of the retail giant. When professors at Georgetown and New York Universities asked Americans in 2018 which institutions they had the most confidence in, only the military ranked higher than \uc2 Amazon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Still, when \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 , the chief executive, testified before Congress last year, he faced accusations that the company squeezes the small businesses that use its online marketplace. A liberal philanthropic organization funded a network of activists to press \uc2 Amazon\uc2  on privacy, competition and labor issues. They have also attacked Mr. Bezos, the richest person in the world by some measures, for his personal wealth.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has made efforts to reach out to the new administration. \uc2 Dave Clark\uc2 , who runs the company's consumer business, sent a letter to the White House in January offering to help with the distribution of the coronavirus vaccine and met virtually with Jeff Zients, the White House's coronavirus coordinator, to discuss the vaccine rollout.\par \par \uc2 In December, the company also hired the lobbyist Jeff Ricchetti, whose brother, Steve Ricchetti, is a longtime aide to Mr. Biden and now a counselor to the president. In the final quarter of last year, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  paid Jeff Ricchetti $60,000, according to disclosure forms he filed with the government.\par \par \uc2 The company's top lobbying and communications executive, \uc2 Jay Carney\uc2 , was a communications director for Mr. Biden during the Obama administration. President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  later named Mr. Carney the White House press secretary. He has deep relationships with Mr. Biden's inner circle, and has played in a garage band with Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Under Mr. Carney's leadership, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has taken steps to satisfy its liberal critics. In 2018, Senator Bernie Sanders, independent of Vermont, attacked the company over its wages. Not long after that, the company announced that it would raise its minimum wage to $15 an hour and push federal policymakers to do the same.\par \par \uc2 ''We listened to our critics, thought hard about what we wanted to do and decided we want to lead,'' Mr. Bezos said in a statement at the time.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has promoted the $15-an-hour minimum in ads in publications frequently used to reach government officials, including Politico and The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . Its lobbyists have pushed for a federal law raising the wage.\par \par \uc2 The union organizers in Bessemer have increasingly attracted support from the White House and top lawmakers in Congress. Labor leaders spent weeks pushing Mr. Biden's staff to have him weigh in on the election at the warehouse.\par \par \uc2 Their lobbying paid off: In February, Mr. Biden appeared in a video that didn't mention \uc2 Amazon\uc2  explicitly but was seen as a clear sign of support to the union. In the video, he said there ''should be no intimidation, no coercion, no threats'' from employers in coming union elections, including in Alabama.\par \par \uc2 ''I didn't speak with the president directly, but my understanding was that the second this hit the president's briefing book, he was like, 'I'm in,''' said Sara Nelson, the president of the Association of Flight Attendants.\par \par \uc2 The Senate majority leader, Chuck Schumer of New York, and Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  of California both support the unionization effort, as do many other Democrats in Congress.\par \par \uc2 ''We've got to protect every worker's right to form and join a union, and to bargain collectively for better pay, quality health care, a safer workplace and a secure retirement,'' said Senator \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2 , a Democrat from Washington, where \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has its main headquarters. ''That absolutely includes the \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers in Alabama, just like workers in Washington State and across our country.''\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's opposition to the union drive could scuttle some of the good will it established among Democrats with its support for a higher federal minimum wage, some liberals said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Sanders said he appreciated \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's help on the minimum wage. ''On the other hand,'' he said, ''to my mind, I find it hard to understand why \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 , the owner of \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and the wealthiest guy in the world, worth about $182 billion, is spending millions of dollars fighting a union organizing effort in Alabama, where his workers are trying to organize for better wages and better working conditions.''\par \par \uc2 But he said he would ultimately approach the company ''issue by issue.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Sanders will appear at a union event in Alabama with the rapper Killer Mike on Friday. Mr. Clark, the \uc2 Amazon\uc2  executive, responded to the news with derision on Wednesday evening.\par \par \uc2 ''I often say we are the Bernie Sanders of employers, but that's not quite right because we actually deliver a progressive workplace to our constituents,'' he said on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It recalled the message \uc2 Amazon\uc2  had waiting for a delegation of progressive lawmakers who met with union representatives in Alabama this month.\par \par \uc2 At the warehouse, workers held up a large banner with text in bold letters: ''CONGRESS: PLEASE MATCH AMAZON'S $15/HOUR MINIMUM WAGE!''\par \par \uc2 Karen Weise contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Karen Weise contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Curtis Gray, vice president of the Retail Wholesale and Department Store Union, outside the Amazon fulfillment warehouse in Bessemer, Ala., this month. (PHOTOGRAPH BY BOB MILLER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B5) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210326eh3q0003d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
