\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Vying for Cuomo's Favor In the 'Belly of the Beast'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brian M. Rosenthal and Luis Ferr\u233\'00\'E9-Sadurn\u237\'00\'ED \par \uc2 1,912 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 March 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dozens of current and former employees of Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo's executive chamber described the office as chaotic, unprofessional and toxic, especially for young women.\par \par \uc2 In the office of Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo, there are usually two annual holiday parties: one, near the New York State Capitol, for all executive employees, and another, at the Governor's Mansion.\par \par \uc2 The smaller soiree is attended mostly by senior administration officials and agency heads, but some aides -- often young women -- also receive invitations.\par \par \uc2 The end-of-year guessing game on who might land invitations has long fueled resentment and speculation, especially among women, who have sometimes wondered if younger aides get invited only if they accede to an office culture still rooted in the ''Mad Men'' era -- including an expectation that they wear high heels to work.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cuomo, a third-term Democrat, is now being investigated by outside lawyers overseen by the state attorney general, and is the subject of an impeachment inquiry after a series of accusations of inappropriate conduct, from unwanted sexual advances to unsolicited kisses and, this week, groping.\par \par \uc2 But as investigators examine those specific allegations, a generation of public servants who have worked under Mr. Cuomo have voiced concerns about the cultural issues that have permeated the state's most powerful workplace on a daily basis.\par \par \uc2 In interviews over the past week, more than 35 people who have worked in Mr. Cuomo's executive chamber described the office as deeply chaotic, unprofessional and toxic, especially for young women.\par \par \uc2 It is a workplace, the current and former employees said, where tasks are assigned not based on job titles, but on who is liked by Mr. Cuomo and his top aides.\par \par \uc2 Those interviewed described an environment where the senior executive staff regularly deride junior workers, test their dedication to the governor and make them compete to earn his affection and avoid his wrath.\par \par \uc2 The workers, for the most part, said they did not personally witness overt sexual harassment. But many said they believed that Mr. Cuomo and other officials seemed to focus on how employees looked and how they dressed. Twelve young women said they felt pressured to wear makeup, dresses and heels, because, it was rumored, that was what the governor liked.\par \par \uc2 One high-ranking current official and two former aides said they believed they had been denied opportunities because they did not dress in the preferred manner.\par \par \uc2 The workplace culture described by the employees is not uncommon in Albany, a state capital with a long history of sexual misconduct scandals and a reputation for after-hours mingling among lobbyists, elected officials and their aides at bars and fund-raisers. But the issues are notable for a governor who has cast himself as a champion for workers and women.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cuomo's office denied many of the issues raised by the employees and said New Yorkers had elected Mr. Cuomo multiple times because ''they know he works day and night for them.''\par \par \uc2 ''There is no secret these are tough jobs, and the work is demanding,'' Richard Azzopardi, a senior adviser, said in a statement. ''But we have a top-tier team with many employees who have been here for years and many others who have left and returned because they know the work we do matters, a fact that was underscored throughout Covid.''\par \par \uc2 Almost all the current and former employees spoke on the condition of anonymity because they feared retaliation from Mr. Cuomo, who is known for threatening critics and whose office has in recent weeks released personnel information about accusers.\par \par \uc2 ''People are frankly still recovering from their experience working there, and they're terrified of speaking out,'' said State Senator Alessandra Biaggi, a former aide and a fierce critic.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Biaggi worked in the executive chamber for seven months in 2017. She said she could not stand the culture, a refrain repeated by several other women who lasted less than a year. ''If you are a woman who wants to focus on work, it is the worst place to be,'' Ms. Biaggi said.\par \par \uc2 Some current and former aides called their time at the office a rewarding experience, praising the governor and criticizing how he had been portrayed by the news media.\par \par \uc2 Others who viewed the workplace culture as problematic said they felt conflicted about working for Mr. Cuomo. They said they still felt a deep pride in the work, and they wondered if the treatment they endured was the price that had to be paid for effective public service.\par \par \uc2 Still, many others said it was not worth it.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cuomo and his top aides regularly set up employees to compete against one other, according to more than two dozen current and former staff members. Some recounted being assigned a task and later discovering a colleague had been sent to the same job.\par \par \uc2 Several recalled having to cut short vacations or miss their children's birthday parties for seemingly minor tasks such as transcribing television interviews with local politicians in other states whom Mr. Cuomo feared could someday become political rivals.\par \par \uc2 They also said Mr. Cuomo and staff members, including his top aide, Melissa DeRosa, would scream and curse at subordinates over small stumbles, like misspelling names. Some said they cried at the office almost every week.\par \par \uc2 Aides who are favored by Mr. Cuomo sit at larger desks, get to travel with him and are invited to pool parties, Super Bowl parties and other events, employees said.\par \par \uc2 The division is evident in the governor's office in the State Capitol, four former aides said. There the employees who are favored -- and comply with the dress code -- sit on one side of the office, in Mr. Cuomo's line of sight. Others sit on the other side.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Azzopardi said that work flow decisions were based on ''what makes sense organizationally,'' noting that some offices were traditionally reserved for specific appointees.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Azzopardi said workers were not hired based on their appearance, and that there was ''not now nor has there ever been an expectation to wear certain clothing or high heels.''\par \par \uc2 The current and former employees said that women -- and particularly young women -- encountered a variety of additional challenges in the office, starting with the hiring process, which several aides said favored women who are tall, thin and blonde.\par \par \uc2 Peter Yacobellis, who served from 2011 to 2014 as the governor's deputy director of administrative services and helped oversee hiring in the Manhattan office, said he often heard inappropriate jokes about the type of women the governor liked to hire.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Yacobellis said that during his tenure, there was no significant training on sexual harassment. He said that void helped foster an environment where senior officials did not know what was appropriate, and junior aides did not know how to report issues.\par \par \uc2 After working at the governor's office, Mr. Yacobellis helped lead the training team in human resources at \uc2 American Express\uc2 . He said he was surprised at the stark differences, and realized how crucial it was for public officials to provide more sexual harassment training in their offices.\par \par \uc2 ''Training could have been the antidote to curing what was clearly a toxic environment for many women in the governor's office,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 The office harassment was not always obvious. Several former employees said there were frequent comments about how aides looked and whom they dated, including from Mr. Cuomo.\par \par \uc2 One former aide said she once overheard Mr. Cuomo learn another aide was pregnant and then joyfully bellow, ''I'm not the father!''\par \par \uc2 Five current and former employees said Mr. Cuomo called them ''darling'' or ''sweetheart.'' Some described it as charming, but others said it made them feel uncomfortable.\par \par \uc2 On Valentine's Day, aides said, Mr. Cuomo sometimes sends roses to aides whom he likes.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cuomo has apologized for some of his behavior around employees, saying he never intended to make anybody feel uncomfortable.\par \par \uc2 Ana Liss, who worked in the office as a policy adviser from 2013 to 2015, said she arrived excited to tackle economic development issues but quickly came to believe Mr. Cuomo valued her for her looks, rather than her work. Ms. Liss is one of several former aides who have publicly accused the governor of inappropriate behavior.\par \par \uc2 The governor, she said, began paying extra attention to her and would often stop at her desk to talk, once kissing her hand. Others took note: At least three colleagues, including a top aide at the time, joked about how the governor found her attractive, she said.\par \par \uc2 ''They said, 'Oh, he likes you,''' she said. ''I took that as a badge of honor, but then I thought that was kind of gross. I thought that I was going there to advance my career as a thought worker. I didn't want to play that weird game.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Liss added, ''Most women can tell when a man is looking at them and for what reason.''\par \par \uc2 Several women who worked for Mr. Cuomo defended him. Three former aides who worked closely with him, including in one-on-one settings, said he never made them feel uncomfortable.\par \par \uc2 Susan Del Percio, a special adviser from 2014 to 2015, said she never observed sexual harassment, though she saw ''an intense environment, a demanding environment, a harsh environment.''\par \par \uc2 Still, many women who did not experience harassment said they believed the women who have come forward in recent weeks. Several said the allegations did not surprise them.\par \par \uc2 The current and former aides also said they had seen other examples of insensitivity toward women, like Mr. Cuomo's aides asking pregnant women to sleep at the office during budget negotiations and a failure to provide enough rooms for breastfeeding mothers.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for Mr. Cuomo said all state government employees worked long hours during budget negotiations, and ''accommodations are readily made for anyone who requires them, whether for pregnancy or any other medical reason.'' He also said there were designated pumping areas for new mothers.\par \par \uc2 Three former employees -- including Ms. Liss -- said they were warned by colleagues that if they wanted to be among the favored aides who received special attention, they had to dress how Mr. Cuomo liked. Nine others said that was implicitly understood.\par \par \uc2 One young former aide who complied with that dress code said she was surprised to often receive high-level assignments. She said that on occasion, she was told she had gotten a task because she was attractive.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Liss said the culture made her feel isolated and intimidated.\par \par \uc2 ''If you work in the belly of the beast, it's a very scary place to be, particularly if you're young, female, na\u239\'00\'EFve, and without a lot of connections,'' she said. ''I have a lot of regrets about not calling it out because I know there are so many other women who had it worse than me.''\par \par \uc2 Emma G. Fitzsimmons, J. David Goodman and Jesse McKinley contributed reporting. Susan C. Beachy contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Emma G. Fitzsimmons, J. David Goodman and Jesse McKinley contributed reporting. Susan C. Beachy contributed research.\par \par \uc2 State Senator Alessandra Biaggi called the governor's office ''the worst place to be'' for a woman. (PHOTOGRAPH BY CINDY SCHULTZ FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Workers said Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo and his top aide, Melissa DeRosa, cursed at them over gaffes. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JUSTIN LANE/EPA, VIA SHUTTERSTOCK) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210313eh3d0003j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
