\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Chief Says \uc2 Costco\uc2  Will Lift Starting Pay to $16 an Hour\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael Corkery \par \uc2 537 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 February 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 W. Craig Jelinek\uc2 , the retailer's chief executive, told lawmakers that the increase was ''a significant competitive advantage for us.''\par \par \uc2 The chief executive of Costco waded into the political debate over the federal minimum wage, testifying at a Senate hearing on Thursday that the retailer was raising its starting pay to $16 an hour.\par \par \uc2 W. Craig Jelinek\uc2 , the chief executive, said \uc2 Costco\uc2 , which already pays an hourly minimum of $15, had some of the highest employee retention rates of any retailer.\par \par \uc2 ''This isn't altruism,'' Mr. Jelinek told the \uc2 Senate Budget Committee\uc2 . ''At \uc2 Costco\uc2 , we know that paying employees good wages and providing affordable benefits makes sense for our business and constitutes a significant competitive advantage for us.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Jelinek was invited to testify by the committee's chairman, Senator Bernie Sanders, a Vermont independent who supports raising the federal minimum wage to $15 an hour over the next four years. Mr. Jelinek said he was not there to endorse the proposed federal minimum wage increase, but rather to discuss how his company's higher-than-average wages had affected his business.\par \par \uc2 Republicans on the committee, including Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  of South Carolina, asked whether \uc2 Costco\uc2 , which generated $150 billion in revenue last year, was able to absorb the higher wages because of its size and whether a $15-an-hour mandate was likely to cause smaller retailers to close.\par \par \uc2 ''In my past experience, wages don't usually put you out of business,'' Mr. Jelinek said. ''It is how you run your business.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Jelinek said the average wage at \uc2 Costco\uc2 , which employs 180,000 people in the United States, was $24 an hour.\par \par \uc2 Costco\uc2 's pay contrasts with that of \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , whose chief executive, \uc2 Doug McMillon\uc2 , declined Mr. Sanders's invitation to testify. \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's average wage is above $15 an hour, but the company's minimum wage is $11 an hour.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Sanders pointed to research by the \uc2 Government Accountability Office\uc2  showing that some \uc2 Walmart\uc2  employees must depend on food stamps and Medicaid to get by on such low wages, calling it a subsidy for the giant corporation.\par \par \uc2 Last week, \uc2 Walmart\uc2  said that it was raising the wages of 425,000 workers and that about half of its work force in the United States would earn at least $15 an hour. But Mr. McMillon stopped short of saying whether the company would eventually extend a $15 minimum to all employees.\par \par \uc2 The chief executive of McDonald's, \uc2 Christopher Kempczinski\uc2 , also declined an invitation to testify Thursday. The chain's average wages are about $12 an hour.\par \par \uc2 Terrence Wise, a \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  worker in Kansas City, Mo., told the committee that he relied on food stamps to raise his three children and had to live out of a minivan after his family was evicted from its home.\par \par \uc2 ''I work for \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 , one of the richest companies in America,'' Mr. Wise said. ''This is what generational poverty looks like. This is what I am fighting to end.''\par \par \uc2 At a Senate hearing, W. Craig Jelinek said offering good pay and benefits ''makes sense'' for Costco. (PHOTOGRAPH BY NATI HARNIK/ASSOCIATED PRESS) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210226eh2q0003j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
