\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Parties Duel Over Relief Aid In a Senate 'Vote-a-Rama'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Luke Broadwater, Hailey Fuchs and Jim Tankersley \par \uc2 1,761 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 February 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Democrats agreed to limit direct checks for high earners but rejected several Republican amendments to a budget resolution key to passing the president's $1.9 trillion rescue plan.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- As lawmakers advanced President Biden's $1.9 trillion stimulus package on Thursday, the Senate dealt a setback to a major tenet of the plan: raising the federal minimum wage to $15 an hour.\par \par \uc2 By a voice vote, senators backed an amendment from Senator \uc2 Joni Ernst\uc2 , Republican of Iowa, to ''prohibit the increase of the federal minimum wage during a global pandemic.'' It was a signal that the wage hike would be difficult to pass in an evenly split Senate, where at least one Democrat, Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin III\uc2  of West Virginia, was on record opposing it.\par \par \uc2 ''A $15 federal minimum wage would be devastating for our hardest-hit small businesses at a time they can least afford it,'' Ms. Ernst said on the Senate floor. ''We should not have a one-size-fits-all policy set by Washington politicians.''\par \par \uc2 The Senate's leading proponent of the $15 minimum wage, Senator Bernie Sanders, independent of Vermont, seemed unfazed. He said that his plan was to carry out the wage increase over five years and that he had never wanted to raise it during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 ''We need to end the crisis of starvation wages in Iowa and around the United States,'' Mr. Sanders said, taking a swipe at Ms. Ernst's home state. He added that he planned to try to get the phased-in wage increase included in a budget reconciliation bill that would allow Mr. Biden's stimulus plan to circumvent the Senate's 60-vote filibuster rule.\par \par \uc2 ''At a time when half of our workers are living paycheck to paycheck, when millions of workers are earning starvation wages and when Congress has not voted to raise the minimum wage since 2007, I will do everything that I can to make sure that a $15-an-hour minimum wage is included in this reconciliation bill,'' Mr. Sanders said.\par \par \uc2 The vote came as Senate Democrats maneuvered through a series of politically tricky amendments that Republicans wanted to attach to a virus relief package and lawmakers pressed forward with a budget plan that includes Mr. Biden's $1.9 trillion economic aid proposal.\par \par \uc2 In a marathon session -- known as a vote-a-rama and for which more than 800 amendments were drafted -- that was expected to stretch into early Friday morning, senators in both parties pushed forward test votes to showcase their dueling priorities. In an evenly split Senate, any amendment required the majority's support to pass, and therefore several failed on a 50-to-50 tie.\par \par \uc2 Among the Republican proposals that failed to garner enough support were measures to reduce funding to states like New York, which is under investigation over coronavirus deaths in nursing homes; to prohibit funding for schools that do not reopen for in-person classes once teachers are vaccinated; and to block funds from so-called sanctuary jurisdictions that do not cooperate with federal law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2 , Democrat of Washington and the chairwoman of the education committee, called the effort to put restrictions on sending aid to schools ''simply a political show.''\par \par \uc2 ''If we withhold funds and schools cannot implement health safety protocols, then we are acting counter to actually getting students back in the classroom,'' Ms. Murray said.\par \par \uc2 Democrats did, however, rally around some amendments from Republicans. The Senate, by unanimous vote, agreed to a motion from Senators \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  and \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , both Republicans of Florida, to block tax increases on small businesses during the pandemic. Lawmakers also backed a measure from Senator Roger Wicker, Republican of Mississippi, and Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 , Democrat of Arizona, to establish a fund to provide grants to food and drinking establishments affected by the coronavirus crisis. And, by a vote of 58 to 42, they agreed to prohibit stimulus money from going to undocumented immigrants -- something that is not included in Mr. Biden's economic rescue plan.\par \par \uc2 The eight Democrats who voted with Republicans on that last measure included John Hickenlooper of Colorado, \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  of New Hampshire, \uc2 Gary Peters\uc2  of Michigan and Mr. Manchin.\par \par \uc2 The Senate also approved an amendment to maintain the U.S. Embassy in Jerusalem. Under President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , the United States recognized Jerusalem as the capital of Israel, breaking with decades of precedent, and opened a new embassy in the city, complicating peace in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Only three lawmakers objected to the amendment on Thursday night: Mr. Sanders, Elizabeth Warren, Democrat of Massachusetts, and \uc2 Thomas R. Carper\uc2 , Democrat of Delaware.\par \par \uc2 On the Senate floor on Wednesday in the lead-up to Thursday's votes, Senator \uc2 Brian Schatz\uc2 , Democrat of Hawaii, dismissed the entire undertaking as a way to ''try to set each other up, that we'll somehow trick someone into taking a bad position that can be turned into a campaign advertisement.''\par \par \uc2 ''Everybody should ignore it if they can. Do anything to not watch vote-a-rama,'' Mr. Schatz said. ''It is boring and it is the worst part of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Among the amendments that passed with bipartisan support -- by a vote of 99 to 1 -- on Thursday was a measure from Mr. Manchin and Senator \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 , Republican of Maine, to restrict $1,400 direct checks included in Mr. Biden's plan from going to high earners, though it did not specify what income level was too high. Democrats have largely agreed to limit payouts for Americans with higher incomes.\par \par \uc2 ''Do we want stimulus checks to go to households with family incomes of $300,000?'' Ms. Collins said.\par \par \uc2 Despite the amendments, the process left Mr. Biden's plan largely intact as Democrats moved forward.\par \par \uc2 ''We cannot repeat the mistakes of the past,'' said Senator Chuck Schumer, Democrat of New York and the majority leader. ''We cannot do too little.''\par \par \uc2 Before the vote, Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  of Kentucky, the Republican leader, had indicated that the amendments were meant to force Democrats into taking a position on some issues they might wish to avoid.\par \par \uc2 ''We're going to put senators on the record,'' he said. ''We'll see how our colleagues vote on these basic, common-sense steps.''\par \par \uc2 The roughly 12-hour rush of vote-casting came as Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  signaled that House Democrats were open to dropping a $15 federal minimum wage from Mr. Biden's plan should the proposal be ruled out of bounds under the Senate's strict budget process rules. Administration officials and some Senate Democrats were bracing for such a possibility.\par \par \uc2 The speaker said Democrats would not give up on trying to raise the wage to $15 an hour if they were forced to jettison it from the stimulus measure.\par \par \uc2 ''It's not the last bill we'll pass,'' Ms. Pelosi said. ''This is the rescue package.''\par \par \uc2 During the voting, Ms. Collins, who led a group of 10 senators who met with Mr. Biden this week with hopes of persuading him to embrace a smaller, $618 billion stimulus package, released a letter to the White House that argued that Mr. Biden was overestimating the money needed to reopen schools and help state and local governments.\par \par \uc2 In an interview, she urged the president to take advantage of money already approved in previous stimulus packages.\par \par \uc2 ''There are hundreds of billions of dollars in unspent funds,'' Ms. Collins said.\par \par \uc2 Democrats were expected to introduce legislation and begin committee debate in the House next week, aiming to move the plan through the budget reconciliation process. They could then circumvent a filibuster, which can only be overcome with 60 votes, and instead pass with a simple majority, allowing the package to be enacted without Republican votes.\par \par \uc2 While details remain in flux, people familiar with the plan said it would largely mirror Mr. Biden's $1.9 trillion proposal. The most significant deviation, they said, was likely to be lowering the cost of providing direct payments to Americans.\par \par \uc2 At Mr. Biden's insistence, the maximum amount of those payments would remain at $1,400. But Democrats and the administration are discussing phasing them out for higher-income Americans at a faster rate than the $600 payments that Congress approved in December, meaning those earning more would get smaller checks.\par \par \uc2 Democrats could further reduce the cost of the plan by lowering the income threshold at which the payments begin to phase out. Mr. Biden has proposed beginning the phaseout for individuals earning $75,000 a year and couples earning $150,000 a year. Lawmakers are discussing reducing those thresholds to $50,000 for individuals and $100,000 for couples, though they have not made a final decision on whether to do so.\par \par \uc2 Among the Republican ideas that appeared to gain some traction with the White House was a proposal from Senator \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  of Utah, who unveiled a plan to send payments of up to $1,250 per month to families with children, in an effort to encourage Americans to have more children while reducing child poverty rates.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Romney's Family Security Act would provide $350 a month for each child up to 5 years old and $250 a month for children ages 6 to 17, via the \uc2 Social Security Administration\uc2 . The payments would be capped at $1,250 per family per month, and they would phase out for individual parents earning above $200,000 a year and couples earning more than $400,000.\par \par \uc2 To offset the costs of the new benefit, Mr. Romney proposed eliminating other government safety net spending, including the Temporary Assistance for Needy Families program and the expanded ''head of household'' deduction for parents who do not itemize their income tax returns.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden's American Rescue Plan includes a one-year expansion of the existing child tax credit and earned-income tax credit, which analysts say could cut child poverty in half. Mr. Romney's plan would streamline the earned-income tax credit, while adding in the child allowance.\par \par \uc2 The plan drew praise as an example of the possibilities of bipartisan action, with the White House chief of staff, Ron Klain, writing in a tweet that it was an ''encouraging sign.''\par \par \uc2 Nicholas Fandos contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Nicholas Fandos contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Speaker Nancy Pelosi signaled a willingness to drop a $15 federal minimum wage proposal from President Biden's stimulus plan. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ANNA MONEYMAKER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210205eh250003p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
